/* precedence — putting versions in the order semver.org says they go, and checking it did.

   `semver` asks whether a version is well-formed. This asks the next question a
   release tool has: given several, which is newest? The spec answers that too,
   in item 11, and gives worked examples — `examples/assets/precedence.txt` holds
   them exactly as written there. Each chain is scrambled, sorted, and compared
   with the spec's own order.

       cufet examples/parsing/precedence.cufe

   The exit contract:

       0   every chain came back in the spec's order
       1   at least one did not
       2   the chain file could not be read

   ── Why this needs a key, and what the key is ──

   ★★ Sorting versions AS TEXT is wrong in two ways the spec's example happens to
   contain both of. `1.0.0-beta.11` sorts before `1.0.0-beta.2`, because `1` is
   less than `2` whatever follows it; and `1.0.0` sorts before `1.0.0-alpha`,
   because a shorter text that is a prefix of a longer one comes first — while
   the spec says a release OUTRANKS its own pre-releases. The program prints the
   text order beside the right one so both mistakes can be seen.

   So the sort is by a KEY: a text built from the version whose ordinary order IS
   precedence. Every rule of item 11 becomes something about characters:

   - Major, minor and patch compare as numbers, so each is padded with zeroes to
     one width. `2` becomes `0000000002`, which now sorts below `0000000011`.
   - A numeric pre-release identifier sorts below an alphanumeric one, so each is
     tagged: `0` before a padded number, `1` before a word.
   - A shorter run of identifiers sorts below a longer one it begins. Each
     identifier is closed with `!`, which is lower than every character an
     identifier may hold — so `alpha!` ends first, and ending first sorts first.
   - A release outranks its pre-releases. The core is followed by `~` for a
     release and `-` for a pre-release, and `~` is higher than `-`.
   - Build metadata does not count, so everything from `+` is dropped.

   ⚠ The width is a limit, and a real one: a number with more than ten digits
   overruns it and sorts wrongly. The spec puts no bound on a version number.

   Exercises `sorted by` a named function and by a function written in place, a
   key function that is a closure over a local, `in reverse`, and a pattern
   called from inside a key function.
*/

Pull a book on regex.
    /* A numeric identifier is digits and nothing else. `converted to number`
       would also accept `-1`, and `-1` is an alphanumeric identifier here: the
       spec counts the hyphen as a letter. */
    Define regex numeric as [^[0-9]+$].

    Define object release with (the text version, the number chain).

    Bind void to usage:
        State "usage: precedence".
        State "".
        State "sorts every chain in examples/assets/precedence.txt and checks it against the spec".
        State "leaves with 0 if they all came back in order, 1 if any did not, 2 on a bad file".
    Done.

    Bind void to play:
        Define target as "examples/assets/precedence.txt".

        If the number of the arguments is greater than 0:
            Cast usage.
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define lines as a series of text.
        Try to:
            The lines becomes read all lines from the file target.
        Done.
        In case of failure:
            State "precedence: {target}: {the message of the failure}".
            Exit with 2.
        Done.

        Define width as 10.

        Bind text to padded, given (the text digits):
            Define out as digits.
            While the length of out is less than width, repeat:
                The out becomes "0" joined to out.
            Done.
            Return out.
        Done.

        Bind text to precedence-key, given (the text version):
            Define bare as item 1 of (version split by "+").
            Define core as bare.
            Define pre as "".
            Define dash as the position of "-" in bare.
            If dash is not void:
                The core becomes the first (dash - 1) characters of bare.
                The pre becomes the characters from (dash + 1) to the end of bare.
            Done.

            Define out as "".
            For each part in core split by ".", repeat:
                The out becomes out joined to cast padded on (part).
            Done.

            If pre is "", return out joined to "~".

            The out becomes out joined to "-".
            For each identifier in pre split by ".", repeat:
                If cast numeric on (identifier):
                    The out becomes out joined to "0" joined to cast padded on (identifier) joined to "!".
                Done.
                Otherwise:
                    The out becomes out joined to "1" joined to identifier joined to "!".
                Done.
            Done.
            Return out.
        Done.

        /* A chain written the way the spec writes one, so a line of output can
           be read against a line of the file. */
        Bind text to chained, given (the series of text versions):
            Define out as "".
            For each version in versions, repeat:
                If the length of out is greater than 0, the out becomes out joined to " < ".
                The out becomes out joined to version.
            Done.
            Return out.
        Done.

        Define chains as 0.
        Define disagreements as 0.
        Define everything as a series of release.

        For each line in lines, repeat:
            If the length of line is 0, skip.
            If the first 1 characters of line is "#", skip.
            Increment chains by 1.

            Define spec-order as line split by " < ".

            /* Scrambled by putting it in REVERSE TEXT order first — the first
               chain is then exactly backwards, and the third comes out in an
               order that is neither the spec's nor its opposite. */
            Define ours as (spec-order sorted in reverse) sorted by precedence-key.

            Define mark as "  ".
            If ours is not spec-order:
                The mark becomes "!!".
                Increment disagreements by 1.
            Done.
            State "{mark} chain {chains converted to text}: {cast chained on (ours)}".

            For each version in spec-order, repeat:
                Insert a new release { the version version, the chain chains } into everything.
            Done.
        Done.

        State "".
        State "{chains converted to text} chain(s) checked against the spec".
        State "{disagreements converted to text} disagreement(s)".

        /* Every version the spec names, newest first. `1.0.0` is in all three
           chains and `1.0.0-alpha` in two; equal versions keep the order they
           were read in, which is what a stable sort promises in either
           direction. */
        State "".
        State "newest first:".
        Define newest as everything sorted by a function given (the release candidate):
            Return cast precedence-key on (candidate's version).
        Done in reverse.
        For each entry in newest, repeat:
            State "  {entry's version} (chain {entry's chain converted to text})".
        Done.

        /* And the order plain text would have given, for comparison. */
        Define names as a series of text.
        For each entry in everything, repeat:
            Insert entry's version into names.
        Done.
        State "".
        State "as text, which is wrong: {cast chained on (names sorted)}".

        If disagreements is greater than 0, exit with 1.
        Exit.
    Done.

    Cast play.
Done.
